sub(r"02_Cosmology_and_Powers\Gods_and_Churches.md", [
 ("They cannot enter Jason's realm or affect his mind in his spirit domains, and they cannot reveal other gods' deceptions.",
  "They cannot see into an astral space that is not part of their world (Book 10: a goddess cannot see into the astral space Jason is holding prisoners in; Book 8: a priest says his god cannot see inside a building hidden from the gods, confirmed). The lorebook adds that they cannot enter Jason's realm or affect his mind in his spirit domains, and cannot reveal other gods' deceptions (unchecked)."),
 ("- They keep a running tally of followers' deaths through the Church of Death.",
  "- The Church of Death certifies deaths: in Book 7 Gary's report is that a certification of Jason's said he had died four times \"so far\", and in Book 10 Jason produces the card for Hana Shavar, \"four deaths, current status: alive\" (both confirmed)."),
 ("Carlos Quilido is a Healer priest and soul healer.", "Carlos Quilido is a Healer priest and soul healer. Hana tells the allies (Book 10, confirmed) that the Church of Undeath lies dormant for generations and strikes when its god has \"saved up his chances to intervene\"."),
 ("Purity, a god of purity, sanctioned himself about five hundred years ago by pouring his authority into a single artefact; a god claiming to be Purity then took over his church.",
  "In the Book 13 briefing Jason explains that Purity, whose nature required him to embody several contradictory meanings, was unstable; about five hundred years ago he was likely to sanction himself, retiring and being reborn. Knowledge interfered, a relic of Purity's divine power was left behind, and a god claiming to be Purity took over his church."),
 ("Over the centuries its doctrine hardened into zealotry, militant orders such as the Order of Redeeming Light, and a doctrine that humans are the \"pure\" state, with rituals that stripped other races of their identity (a modified vampiric curse, per Carlos's investigation). Carlos concludes Purity is a fraud; Disguise, a trickster god, gives up the pretence and reveals he was masquerading as Purity. Knowledge helped him (Book 13 briefing).",
  "Book 10 (confirmed) states that the Order of Redeeming Light was created by Disguise, masquerading as Purity: its Flames of Redemption are an extreme modification of lesser vampirism, and the order was founded only after Disguise took on Purity's mantle, which fits Disguise's method of whitewashing vampirism as redemption. Over the centuries the doctrine hardened into zealotry and a belief that humans are the \"pure\" state (lorebook). Jason tells Melody Jain that her god is \"an illusion. A disguise.\" Carlos worked out that Purity was a fraud (lorebook); Knowledge helped Disguise (Book 13 briefing)."),
 ("A third of her priests are her spies. She tears Gabrielle Pellin's divine essence out in front of Jason. (Lorebook summary of Book 13; unchecked.)",
  "The briefing (Book 13, confirmed in part) says Knowledge has been preparing to invade Earth for half a millennium and used Jason as a key part of her plan, that she promised the messengers' leader a chance to invade Pallimustus and claim Purity's relic, and that around a third of the priests and priestesses of Knowledge are her spies. When Gabrielle Pellin refuses to believe it, a cube leaves her chest and passes into Knowledge's hand and Gabrielle has a seizure (confirmed); Travis catches her, and her divine essence is gone. The goddess becoming \"just God\" of Earth, the role of Disguise in recruiting the Network's founder, and the remaining details are the lorebook's account and are unchecked."),
 ("The goddess of Knowledge appears as a Mediterranean-looking woman who knows everything anyone in her world knows.",
  "The goddess of Knowledge appears as a woman with colourful clothes and Mediterranean features, beautiful yet \"detached and untouchable\", in Jason's first meeting with her in Book 1 (confirmed); the lorebook adds that she knows everything anyone in her world knows."),
 ("She offers a pact: he may never resurrect himself or anyone else, but he gains the title Keeper of the Cycle, undeath energy is excluded from his realm and aura, and ghost fire is added to his afflictions. In Book 12 she returns his stored resurrection power. (Lorebook; unchecked.)",
  "She appears as a stocky, middle-aged woman in a colourful dress with the warmth of a matronly tavern keeper (Book 10, confirmed). The pact seals any ability to resurrect himself or another, persists through any changes in his nature, and is enacted by the goddess guiding his soul to make changes to itself, without gaining access to it. In return he gains the title Keeper of the Cycle; abilities that apply afflictions also apply ghost fire, mana drain degrades the animating force of undead, and allies in his aura are affected by ghost fire while he suppresses the undead (Book 10, confirmed). In Book 12 she returns his stored resurrection power (lorebook; unchecked)."),
 ("Gary drinks the cup in Book 10 and dies about a year later; Hero later sanctions himself. (Wiki and lorebook; the wiki's detail about the confluence is unconfirmed in the book text.)",
  "Gary drinks the cup in Book 10; in Book 11 Gary says \"I'm only a demigod\" and Gwydion tells him that Hero has decided to sanction himself, something he has considered for longer than anyone has been alive (Book 11, confirmed). The wiki's detail that the confluence becomes a Hero essence is unconfirmed in the book text, and Gary's death about a year later is a lorebook claim."),
])

sub(r"02_Cosmology_and_Powers\Astral_Kings_and_Messengers.md", [
 ("- **Original astral kings** guided messengers when they came into being, as a parent guides a child. Marek's people recall (Book 11, confirmed) that originally there was \"no obedience, no alien eye inside our souls\": freedom rather than servitude. The brand and the seal came later.",
  "- **Original astral kings** guided messengers when they came into being, as a parent guides a child; they were not themselves messengers. Boris explains (Book 11, confirmed) that back then there were no limits on rank, because rank limits are a function of the brand, which did not exist. When messengers transcended and became astral kings, they instituted the brand and enslaved their own kind; war and rebellion followed, the free lost, and the defeated went into hiding. The Unorthodoxy descends from the free."),
 ("- **Nature** (lorebook, partly confirmed): gestalt beings whose body and soul are one thing; Jason explains (Book 9, confirmed) that because auras are projections of the soul, their gestalt nature gives them aura abilities that body-soul dual beings do not have. Appearance (lorebook): tall, about one and a half times celestine height, winged, alabaster skin with solid gold eyes.",
  "- **Nature**: gestalt beings whose body and soul are one thing; Jason explains (Book 9, confirmed) that because auras are projections of the soul, their gestalt nature gives them aura abilities that body-soul dual beings do not have. A messenger seen in Book 9 looks like a celestine with alabaster skin, long spun-gold hair and solid gold eyes, but stands some eight feet tall and has wings (confirmed). Messengers can hide their wings, reduce their size and diminish their beauty (Book 13, confirmed), and have hidden on Earth for millennia."),
 ("- **Belief**: they consider themselves the highest life in the cosmos and the servants of astral kings; their message is \"Kneel\" (lorebook).",
  "- **Belief and the instinct to kneel**: Book 10 has a chapter titled \"The Instinct to Kneel\" in which Hess confesses to feeling it; that messengers consider themselves the highest life in the cosmos and that their message is \"Kneel\" are lorebook claims."),
 ("- **Rank**: the lorebook says they are capped at silver by a seal that siphons their potential into the astral king, that Voices of the Will can reach gold, and that diamond messengers may become astral kings. In Book 9 text, Fal Vin Garath is a silver-rank messenger master and Jes Fin Kaal is a gold-rank Voice of the Will (confirmed).",
  "- **Rank**: the books confirm that rank limits are a function of the brand (Boris, Book 11), that Fal Vin Garath is a silver-rank messenger master and Jes Fin Kaal a gold-rank Voice of the Will (Book 9). The lorebook's specific claim that messengers are capped at silver by a seal that siphons their potential, and that diamond messengers may become astral kings, is unchecked."),
 ("- **Resurrection**: does not work on messengers (lorebook).", "- **Resurrection**: does not work on messengers or anyone like them; their life is not just physical and is tenacious (Jason, Book 9, confirmed)."),
 ("- **Birth**: messengers are \"budded\" like plants (Book 12, lorebook).", "- **Birth**: the lorebook says messengers are \"budded\" like plants (unchecked)."),
 ("Dissenting messengers, led by Marek Nior Vargas (a defector from Book 9), want to live without astral kings. Some are freed by Jason (Tera Jun Casta, Jali Corrik Fen) and live in his realm. Boris Ket Lundi leads Unorthodoxy messengers on Earth. (Lorebook; names confirmed in Books 9-10.)",
  "Dissenting messengers who want to live without astral kings. Boris Ket Lundi tells Jason (Book 11, confirmed) that he is Unorthodoxy and that his astral king's allies do not know; Book 12 confirms that Navise Den Rigal, a Voice of the Will, serves a minor astral king who is secretly one of the Unorthodoxy's rare astral kings, and that Boris was slipped into the service of Vesta Carmis Zell. Marek Nior Vargas leads a breach force against Yaresh in Book 9, has misgivings about the operation and the politics, and later brings his people into Jason's realm; whether Marek leads the Unorthodoxy is unchecked. Tera Jun Casta is a branded messenger whose brand Jason removes (Book 10, confirmed: it was the brand of her astral king master, Vesta Carmis Zell, and it traumatises her)."),
 ("Jamis Fran Muskar appears in Book 10 as a messenger speaking for a larger body (the lorebook calls him a Council of Kings avatar).",
  "The Council of Kings is, in Book 10 (confirmed), \"the closest thing the messengers had to central leadership\"; a group of prime avatars, each belonging to a different member, watches the Builder's agents preparing to move a planet. Many astral kings stand outside it (Book 11, confirmed). Jamis Fran Muskar appears as a messenger speaking for a larger body (the lorebook calls him a Council of Kings avatar)."),
])

